1
00:05:19,090 --> 00:05:25,090
A Questlove Jawn.

2
00:10:38,181 --> 00:10:44,181
Summer of Soul (...Or, When the Revolution Could Not Be Televised) is a
music, documentary, history film directed by Questlove.

3
00:15:57,272 --> 00:16:03,272
During the same summer as Woodstock, over 300,000 people attended the Harlem Cultural Festival,
celebrating African American music and culture, and promoting Black pride and unity.

4
00:21:16,363 --> 00:21:22,363
The footage from the festival sat in a basement, unseen for over 50 years,
keeping this incredible event in America's history lost — until now.

5
00:26:35,454 --> 00:26:41,454
Self, played by Stevie Wonder.

6
00:31:54,545 --> 00:32:00,545
Self - Artist, played by Lin-Manuel Miranda.

7
00:37:13,636 --> 00:37:19,636
Self - Artist, played by Chris Rock.

8
00:42:32,727 --> 00:42:38,727
Self - Harlem Cultural Festival Producer & Host
(archive footage), played by Tony Lawrence.

9
00:47:51,818 --> 00:47:57,818
Self (archive footage), played by Nina Simone.

10
00:53:10,909 --> 00:53:16,909
Self (archive footage), played by B.B. King.

11
00:58:29,999 --> 00:58:35,999
Summer of Soul (...or, When the Revolution Could Not Be Televised) is a 2021 American independent documentary film
about the 1969 Harlem Cultural Festival, directed by Ahmir "Questlove" Thompson in his directorial debut.

12
01:03:49,090 --> 01:03:55,090
It had its world premiere at the 2021 Sundance Film Festival on January 28, 2021, where it
won the Grand Jury Prize and Audience Award in the documentary categories.

13
01:09:08,181 --> 01:09:14,181
It had a limited theatrical release in the U.S.

14
01:14:27,272 --> 01:14:33,272
by Searchlight Pictures on June 25, 2021, before expanding and
being released for streaming on Hulu the next weekend.

15
01:19:46,363 --> 01:19:52,363
The second half of the movie's title is taken from the 1970 poem and song "The
Revolution Will Not Be Televised", written by Gil Scott-Heron.

16
01:25:05,454 --> 01:25:11,454
It offered a sharp and satirical critique of the media's lack of coverage of civil rights activism and the
reality of change and revolution taking place in the streets and on campuses across America.

17
01:30:24,545 --> 01:30:30,545
The film received acclaim from critics, with particular praise
given to the restoration of the footage used.

18
01:35:43,636 --> 01:35:49,636
It won numerous awards, including Best Documentary Feature at the 6th Critics' Choice Documentary Awards, where it won in all six categories in which it was nominated, Best
Documentary at the 75th British Academy Film Awards, Best Documentary Feature at the 94th Academy Awards, and Best Music Film at the 64th Annual Grammy Awards.

19
01:41:02,727 --> 01:41:08,727
It has been considered one of the best films of the 21st century.

20
01:46:21,818 --> 01:46:27,818
The film explores themes of jazz, empowerment, blues,
civil rights, rhythm and blues (r&b).

21
01:51:40,909 --> 01:51:46,909
The tone is lighthearted.
